BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Play Dead
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260625T200000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260628T160000
UID:495db7d93771795f1d9f61e3994ddf88@toronto.ca
DESCRIPTION:Play Dead invites you into a distorted world where the familia
 r twists into something wonderful and strange. Everyday rituals warp into 
 surreal scenes\, exposing the desperation that underpins our search for co
 nnection. In this gravity-defying universe\, bodies collide and tangle\, c
 aught between beauty and menace — both intimate and unsettling. Montreal
 's People Watching crafts a vivid blend of acrobatics\, movement\, and hig
 h octane circus that magnifies the absurdity of the everyday. These moment
 s\, strange yet achingly familiar\, mirror our own desire to hold on\, lik
 e couples not wanting the last dance to end. Haunting and exhilarating\, P
 lay Dead captures the fragility\, absurdity\, and fleeting wonder of life 
 lived on the edge of connection. La Presse lauds the performance as "Maste
 rful fluidity\, breathtaking…the circus of tomorrow".
LOCATION:Meridian Arts Centre\, 5040 Yonge St\, North York\, ON
URL:https://www.luminatofestival.com/play-dead
X-SOURCE:City of Toronto Festivals & Events
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Toronto Swahili Summer Festival
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260711T090000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260711T090000
UID:3b45427b8d55c2f8f3c73046a6726746@toronto.ca
DESCRIPTION:An annual cultural event that celebrates the Swahili language 
 and the diverse heritage of East African communities. The festival is orga
 nized by the Swahili Cultural Centre and provides a platform for people to
  experience Swahili culture through music\, food\, fashion\, art and more.
LOCATION:163 Queens Street Wast\, 163 Queen St E\, Toronto\, ON
URL:www.swahiliculturalcenter.com
X-SOURCE:City of Toronto Festivals & Events
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:iHarpFest 2026 Toronto International Harp Festival
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260711T093000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260712T100000
UID:59e26e18ab6c1255012099e8eb641048@toronto.ca
DESCRIPTION:World heritage. Canada's harmony. Experience a week of a globa
 l gathering of harpists\, educators\, and music lovers celebrating the har
 p's timeless beauty and boundless future. We're bathering the world's most
  distinguished harpists together at two of Toronto's most inspiring venues
 \, the Aga Khan Museum and The Ismaili Centre for a celebration of culture
 \, creativity\, and connection. From Queen Victoria to Broadway settings\,
  from ancient strings to contemporary sounds\, Toronto will be transformed
  into the most diverse international stage for the harp\, the world's most
  ethereal instrument.
LOCATION:The Ismaili Center Toronto - Social Hall\, 49 Wynford Dr\, Toront
 o\, ON
URL:www.iharpfest.com
X-SOURCE:City of Toronto Festivals & Events
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Threads of Connection and Conversation
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260903T100000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261002T100000
UID:fc9c2a723d831ff491b56bf7fc3a6067@toronto.ca
DESCRIPTION:Fleeting moments and beauty of urban life captured through vis
 ual storytelling inviting viewers to pause\, connect and reflect. Anchored
  in connection\, this exhibition preserves the essence of human interwoven
 ness. By Isabel Fryszberg\, Janna Robins Walters and Poonam Khanna.
LOCATION:Assembly Hall Gallery\, 1 Colonel Samuel Smith Park Dr\, Etobicok
 e\, ON
URL:https://www.isabelfryszberg.com/artwork
X-SOURCE:City of Toronto Festivals & Events
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:How to Survive a Bear Attack by Claire Cameron - By the Lake Book 
 Club
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260915T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260915T190000
UID:2f02cbbbe586396d614f9fc67eac3ea6@toronto.ca
DESCRIPTION:How to Survive a Bear Attack\, by Claire Cameron\, finalist fo
 r the 2025 Governor General's Literary Award in Non-Fiction\, investigates
  a rare bear attack in Algonquin Park while reflecting on her own battle w
 ith genetic cancer. Blending nature writing\, true crime and memoir\, Came
 ron explores fear\, resilience and survival. By the Lake Book Club hosts s
 ix Tuesday-night events from February to December with top Canadian author
 s and Humber Polytechnics alumni. Each in-person event features readings\,
  discussions\, Q&A\, book signings and ASL interpretation\, while virtual 
 sessions include live subtitles and audio access.
LOCATION:Assembly Hall\, 1 Colonel Samuel Smith Park Dr\, Etobicoke\, ON
URL:https://secure.toronto.ca/webapps/feps_edc-ticketing/#events/c4cac11e-
 531e-4bb5-b8e0-2ed0a3d542fb
X-SOURCE:City of Toronto Festivals & Events
END:VEVENT
